Introduction 
Modern automobiles are being equipped with ever more innovative technology, as the industry develops rapidly. Augmented reality is a tool to enhance people’s daily life, recreation, and work-related activities. There are several distinctive solutions for automotive augmented reality (AR), including augmented reality head-up display (AR-HUD), which is able to project road tips, signs, and warnings on the windshield in real-time [1]. AR-HUD offers outstanding advantages in terms of improved driving experience and enhanced road safety. 
The idea of using HUD as an Augmented Reality device was originally introduced and explored for avionic applications [2], [3]. While an automotive AR-HUD system is an augmented reality display that utilizes the windshield as an optical combiner. The projector system projects the roads’ navigation information or other necessary information on the free-form surface combiner through another pair of free-form surface reflectors, and the driver obtains the fusion information from the virtual image and the world outside. Therefore, drivers are no longer required to bow their heads and turn their attention to mobile information. They can always be aware of road conditions and respond to all kinds of emergencies more quickly, which increases both safety and comfort. 
However, in AR-HUDs, if the user views the virtual image from different perspectives, the image will exhibit varying degrees of distortion and overall translation, as is illustrated in Fig. 1(a). The inaccessibility of the retinal image signifies an additional challenge for the multi-directional dynamic calibration of AR-HUDs [4]. Before it is put into use, the one-to-one correspondence between the human eye position and distortion must be calibrated in a particular manner. During use, the eye-tracking camera obtains eye position [5], [6], [7], and the real-time dynamic distortion correction is carried out according to the offline calibration, as shown in Fig. 1(b). Also, the high degree of freedom of the eye position, the complex projector, and the large virtual image size are factors that impede calibration. The complexity and unreliability of the calibration procedures are the major obstacles to the widespread adoption of AR-HUDs in industrial applications. Also, the dynamic alignment between the projected image and the real world (monocular motion disparity) is also one of the issues to be solved. The sum of the effects of the above two issues (distortion and monocular motion disparity) is regarded as a generalized distortion. 
Therefore, better calibration is vital for creating a safer and more comfortable driving environment, but it also faces tremendous challenges. 
This section outlines some related work and discusses why our solution can be superior to similar ones. 
Fixed viewpoint calibration. In the early stages of the development of AR-HUDs, distortion correction was primarily achieved by the design of two free-form surfaces in the projector [8], [9], [10]. By designing a free-form surface reflection system suitable for the shape of the windshield, the distortion is minimized at a certain viewpoint. Later, Wu et al. [11] constructed an interior system focusing primarily on distortion correction with fixed angle viewing and fixed head positioning on the basis of pre-warped points and interpolation. These methods correct the distortion in a fixed viewpoint. When people change positions, distortion and disparity will always happen, resulting in a bad effect of correction. 
Traditional dynamic calibration. Wientapper et al. [12] first proposed dynamic distortion correction for vehicle AR-HUD system. They correct the optical distortion using a polynomial regression model. It is limited to a small display area, and the process of calibration involves complex components. Therefore, some subsequent work has been supplemented on this basis [13] or put forward better improvement schemes [14], [15], [16]. Basically, these methods establish and store a relationship table of pre-distortion maps for some eye positions, and then use an interpolation method for the other eye positions. This interpolation method is now widely used by the industry to correct distortion [14]. 
A substantial amount of data is required to form the look-up table, which has high requirements for the storage of the system, and the interpolation of distortion models in different viewpoints will inevitably lead to the hopping of distortion correction [17] and negatively impact the user experience. In the process of converting a distortion map into a pre-distortion map, surface fitting and inverse transformation methods are utilized, and the improvement in fitting accuracy leads to an exponential increase in calculation time. To date, the projection virtual image resolution is still low (about 400 × 800 pixels). As resolution increases, the amount of data will need more storage and processing, and it is more difficult to achieve better overall image distortion correction. 
Dynamic calibration based on deep learning. Due to the shortcomings of traditional methods, we propose the method of constructing a fully connected neural network. HUDNet can be used to predict the pre-distortion warping map according to each input eye position to achieve image correction. A neural network method offers superior generalization abilities and processing efficiency as compared to the traditional method. At the same time, it is helpful for eliminating the problems caused by interpolation. The application prospects are exciting. In comparison to Li’s [17], ours puts forward new ideas including position coding, subfield prediction, and disparity decoupling. By exploring the properties of neural networks, we can achieve better results on this task. 
Other works. Optical See-Through Head-Mounted-Displays (OSTHMD) share many similarities with HUDs as they also use a translucent reflector as a combination to create virtual images in front of the eyes. There are several methods [18], [19], [20], [21] available for estimating projective properties. Most of these methods rely on user interaction, in which several virtual points must be aligned with the actual points in the scene. As the eye position in an HMD is fixed, it does not provide a useful reference for the HUD. 
In this paper, we propose a deep-learning solution to predict the one-to-one correspondence between eye position and distortion. When calibrating offline, a standard lattice is projected. Inspired by Owen et al. [22], we use an auxiliary camera mounted on a mechanical arm to replace the human eye, and collect the distorted images of the lattice from various viewpoints. A dataset can be obtained for training HUDNet by recognizing the lattice images. The generalized distortion is corrected directly as a whole in the previous works. However, because the variation of parallax is large, the variation of distortion is complex, which will increase the error of generalized distortion prediction. We decouple disparity information from distortion information, which greatly reduces the difficulty of distortion prediction. Furthermore, based on our analysis, we find that the lattice distortion variable in the center of the image is extremely small. Consequently, if it is predicted directly by the neural network, more distortion will be introduced as a result of prediction error. We, therefore, present a more accurate method for predicting the distortion of the central field of view. 
In summary, our contribution includes the following: 
 
    (i) 
    We propose a three-input HUDNet based on position coding. To achieve higher accuracy of prediction, the network outputs both the disparity and distortion separately. Our study investigates the relationship between the properties of neural networks and the task of distortion prediction. 
    (ii) 
    We propose a progressive data pre-processing strategy. This is the first attempt at decoupling disparity information from distortion information and using the percentage of distortion to the FOV to predict the small distortion in the center of the field of view. In the distortion correction work, for the first time, we consider the influence of the disparity problem on the alignment of the projected image and the real scene, which provides a better interface for the subsequent process. 
 
In Section 2, we present the specific conceptual foundations of our proposed method. Section 3 outlines the results, verifying the effectiveness and feasibility of our method. The major lessons learned thus far are summarized in the concluding Section 4. 
